    <h3>The "Read Before You Cite" Contract</h3>
    <p>The protocol is explicit about what constitutes a "loaded" rule. Three conditions must all be met:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><code>read_file</code> was called and returned successfully (verifiable in tool trace)</li>
      <li>The guidance from the rule is applied to the response content</li>
      <li>The rule is declared as a Gate 3 sub-bullet in the PRE-FLIGHT header</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This is the central invariant that the eval harness checks. When a model claims a rule is loaded (condition 3) without a corresponding <code>read_file</code> call (condition 1) in the tool trace, that is a protocol violation&mdash;specifically, failure mode FM-1 (<em>declared-but-not-read</em>).</p>

    <h3>Rules vs. Reference Files</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h4>Rules (loaded + cited)</h4>
        <p>Files under <code>rules/</code> with numeric prefixes (e.g., <code>100-snowflake-core.md</code>). These are the domain-specific guidance documents that models must read and cite under Gate 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Reference Files (discovery aids)</h4>
        <p>Files used to <em>find</em> rules, not loaded as rules themselves. These include <code>AGENTS.md</code> (the bootstrap protocol) and <code>RULES_INDEX.md</code> (the keyword index).</p>
        <p>Reference files must NOT appear under Gate 3.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The contract is simple: discover, read, cite, respond. Every failure mode in this report is a deviation from one of these four steps.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why This Design?</h3>
    <p><strong>Verifiability.</strong> Each step produces an observable artifact. The eval harness doesn't need to understand <em>what</em> the model read&mdash;it only needs to confirm <em>that</em> it read.</p>
    <p><strong>Freshness guarantee.</strong> Without the read requirement, a model could "comply" with the protocol using stale knowledge. The <code>read_file</code> call forces the current version into context.</p>
    <p><strong>Composability.</strong> The protocol adapts to different runtime environments: primary path uses a delegated skill; fallback uses basic grep. Both converge on the same terminal state.</p>

    <h3>Key Terminology</h3>
    <details>
      <summary>Glossary of terms used in this report</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fixture:</strong> A synthetic user prompt designed to test specific rule-loading paths.</li>
        <li><strong>Gate:</strong> A checkpoint in the protocol sequence with a verifiable artifact.</li>
        <li><strong>PRE-FLIGHT header:</strong> The structured block declaring which gates passed and which rules were loaded.</li>
        <li><strong>Tool trace:</strong> The complete sequence of tool calls made by the model during a single run.</li>
        <li><strong>Parametric memory:</strong> Knowledge encoded in the model's weights during training.</li>
        <li><strong>Stochastic instability:</strong> Identical inputs producing different compliance outcomes across runs due to sampling variance.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
